**2. Using `strtod` (C-Style, More Control, Catches Underflow Better)**

If you want to avoid exceptions or need more reliable detection of underflow, you can use the C standard library function `strtod`. It reports range errors by setting the global `errno` variable to `ERANGE`.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <cstdlib>   // For strtod
#include <cerrno>    // For errno, ERANGE
#include <cmath>     // For HUGE_VAL
#include <cctype>    // For isspace

bool isDoubleOutOfRange_strtod(const std::string& numStr, double& outValue) {
    char* endPtr;
    errno = 0; // IMPORTANT: Reset errno before calling strtod

    const char* cstr = numStr.c_str();
    outValue = std::strtod(cstr, &endPtr);

    // Check 1: Did any conversion happen?
    if (endPtr == cstr) {
        // String was empty or did not start with a valid number format
        return true; // Treat as invalid/error
    }

    // Check 2: Was the entire string consumed (ignoring trailing whitespace)?
    // Skip trailing whitespace
    while (std::isspace(static_cast<unsigned char>(*endPtr))) {
        endPtr++;
    }
    if (*endPtr != '\0') {
        // Trailing non-whitespace characters found
        return true; // Treat as invalid/error if full string match required
    }

    // Check 3: Did a range error occur (overflow or underflow)?
    if (errno == ERANGE) {
        // The value was too large (outValue == HUGE_VAL or -HUGE_VAL)
        // or too small (outValue == 0.0 and underflow occurred).
        return true; // Out of range
    }

    // If we get here, conversion was successful and within range.
    return false;
}

